KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ disjunction-constrained conversion operator: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*is unknown.*$
--
[temp.constr.op]/[temp.deduct]/5: a conversion-function template whose
requires-clause is a *disjunction* (integral<T> || floating_point<T>) must be
removed from the candidate set when the whole constraint is unsatisfied with the
deduced arguments.

KNOWNBUG: the conversion-template constraint check folds only a single atomic
constraint (via typecheck_expr); it does not evaluate the boolean structure of a
disjunction/conjunction, so or(false, false) is not recognised as unsatisfied
and the non-viable candidate survives, producing a spurious
"operator <class> is unknown".

Minimal model of libstdc++'s __max_size_type / __max_diff_type conversion
operators (constraint integral<_Tp> || __is_int128<_Tp>), reached when
elaborating __normal_iterator's C++20 iterator_concept member.  Reclassify CORE
once constraint-satisfaction evaluation handles the boolean operators.
